      "screen": 3,
      "kind": "unclear",
      "severity": "minor",
      "problem": "The reason for 11 °C says \"You took the start away instead of adding the rise to it.\" A beginner who picked 11 added 4 and 7 and ignored that the start is below zero. Taking the start away is not what that learner did. It also contradicts the screen's own idea that subtracting gives −11.",
      "fix": "Change the reason to: \"You added 4 and 7 as if the start were above zero, but −4 °C is below zero.\""
    },
    {
      "screen": 4,
      "kind": "filler",
      "severity": "minor",
      "problem": "This explanation (\"Zero is just a mark on the way up. Count the degrees to zero, then the degrees after it.\") only repeats what screens 2 and 3 already showed. It adds no new idea or example.",
      "fix": "Add a new example with different numbers, such as −2 °C plus a 6 degree rise (2 degrees to zero, then 4 more, so 4 °C), or merge the point into screen 3 and drop this screen."
    },
    {
      "screen": 1,
      "kind": "unclear",
      "severity": "minor",
      "problem": "The content has an \"exampleLineSlot\" whose idea reads \"A cold morning where the learner lives.\" This looks like a writer instruction rather than learner-facing content, and it is not an example with numbers.",
      "fix": "Replace it with a concrete example line, e.g. \"On a cold morning in Spokane it's −3 °C. A rise of 3 degrees brings it to 0 °C.\", or remove the slot."
    },
    {
      "screen": null,
